1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:13,500
A short circuit can unleash enormous current! Here, two sources feed
the fault. The limiter acts on source one. Source two connects
directly to the downstream bus.

2
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:20,800
In normal operation, the reactor stays in series. The capacitor
compensates its reactance.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:31,500
The thyristors are blocked. At sixty hertz, inductive and capacitive
reactances nearly cancel, keeping the normal voltage drop small.

4
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:39,400
Now watch the fault response in slow motion. Initially, current
flows through the capacitor.

5
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:47,000
Detection takes zero point three seven milliseconds. Conduction
starts after zero point four eight milliseconds.

6
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:57,500
Current transfers to the bypass, removing capacitor compensation.
The reactor now limits source one's fault contribution. It was
connected all along!

7
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:05,300
Compare phase A on identical axes: red without the limiter, blue
with it.

8
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:15,000
Symmetrical R M S fault current falls from seventy-nine point nine
six to forty-nine point nine one kiloamps.

9
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:19,600
That's a thirty-seven point five eight percent reduction!

10
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:29,000
The blue trace is three-phase R M S, including D C. The feeder
breaker opens.

11
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,300
Removing gate pulses does not force turn-off. Each thyristor needs
its own current zero.

12
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:40,100
Once conduction ends and recovery is ready, the feeder can reclose.

13
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:49,500
The magnified trace reveals a damped transient. Current returns to
normal, with capacitor compensation restored.

14
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:57,600
A larger reactor reduces fault current. These five points come from
simulated cases.

15
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The capacitor is retuned at every point for sixty hertz. Reactor
size sets limitation; capacitor tuning sets normal compensation.
